SPU: a DMA GET finds its overlay source through a hash of EA and signature instead of a scan of the table.

spu_overlay_note_get runs on every MFC GET and compared the transfer with every registered overlay source.
Resistance 3 registers ~220 (215 Insomniac job modules by EA, 3 WWS job modules by signature), and most GETs are
data that match none: the scan was ~6% of the SPU threads' time in a firefight (`sample`). The sources are now
hashed by EA and, for the signature ones, by their 16 bytes; each bucket keeps registration order and a lookup
merges the EA and signature candidates in that order, so the source that takes a GET is the one the scan
found first. spu_overlay_match_sig uses the signature buckets too. Registration happens before the SPUs run.

Test: runtime/spu/tests/test_spu_lifted_start.c, test_overlay_source_order (two sources on one EA, one lifted
for another load address; a signature and an EA source in either order; the 512-byte rule for signatures;
match_sig), 178 checks, the same with the old scan; taking EA candidates first fails two. Micro-benchmark with
the game's source mix: 72 -> 7.5 ns per GET.
In the game (`sample`, 25 s of the Haven firefight, 2 runs each): spu_overlay_note_get 627/904 of ~11.7k SPU
samples (5.3-7.8%) -> 107/87 of ~12.4k (0.7-0.9%); the runtime's share of the SPU threads 23-27% -> 19-20%.

Upstream: not proposed yet.
---

